=Generate a {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.guideType }} technical guide.

GUIDE DETAILS:
- Topic: {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.topic }}
- Audience: {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.audience === 'customer' ? 'Customer-Facing (professional tone, business value focus, avoid internal jargon)' : 'Internal SE (technical depth, implementation details, include config notes)' }}
- Industry: {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.industry || 'Not specified' }}
- Tech Stack: {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.techStack || 'Not specified' }}
- Additional Context: {{ $('Webhook').item.json.body.customerContext || 'None provided' }}

---

ATLAS DOCUMENTATION:
{{ $('Merge Contexts').item.json.docContext }}

---
{{ $('Merge Contexts').item.json.hasSME ? '\nFIELD KNOWLEDGE FROM SE TEAMS CHANNEL (real-world Q&A from Atlas SMEs â use to add field-validated nuance, deployment gotchas, and customer objection handling):\n' + $('Merge Contexts').item.json.smeContext + '\n\n---\n' : '' }}
Write a complete, polished technical guide in markdown. Be specific â reference the actual tech stack and industry provided. Incorporate field knowledge where relevant to add real-world depth beyond what the documentation covers.

Return the guide as JSON:
{ "guide": "<full markdown guide here>" }

parameters.jsCode:
const raw = $input.first().json.content[0].text;
const cleaned = raw.replace(/^```json\n/, '').replace(/\n```$/, '').trim();

let guide;
try {
  const parsed = JSON.parse(cleaned);
  guide = parsed.guide;
} catch (e) {
  // If Claude returned raw markdown without JSON wrapping, use as-is
  guide = raw;
}

// Pass through jobId and callbackUrl from the original webhook payload
const jobId = $('Webhook').item.json.body.jobId || null;
const callbackUrl = $('Webhook').item.json.body.callbackUrl || null;

return [{ json: { guide, jobId, callbackUrl } }];
